# Web and mobile parity

Gocha is one React Native app shipped to iOS, Android, and the web (https://gocha.ai/) from `mobile/gocha-app/`. Web is not a separate product, demo, or degraded shell. Treat every prompt as requiring full parity unless the owner explicitly scopes to one platform.

## Default assumption

- If you build or change a screen, flow, button, input, loader, search, or API call, it must work on web and on native the same way.
- Do not ship mobile-first and "fix web later." Do not leave web broken while calling the task done.
- Backend is shared (`app_gocha/`). The same Laravel API serves web and mobile.

## Implementation

- Source lives in `mobile/gocha-app/`. Web builds with `npm run build:web` (Vite + react-native-web).
- Prefer shared components and hooks. Use `*.web.tsx` / `*.web.ts` only for DOM-specific mechanics (native `<button>`, `onClick`, keyboard events) when React Native `Pressable` or primitives are unreliable on web. Behavior and UX must match across platforms.
- Do not gate features with `Platform.OS === 'web'` to skip functionality. Platform checks are for implementation details only, not product differences.
- Keyboard and pointer: wire Enter/submit, click targets, and focus on web the same as tap/submit on mobile. Verify interactive controls actually fire on web (Pressable alone is not enough for critical actions).

## Verification before push

- Run `npm test` and `npm run build:web` from `mobile/gocha-app/`.
- Smoke the changed flow on https://gocha.ai/ (hard refresh after deploy), not only native simulators.
- If you touch auth, search, chat, or forms, click/type through the real web UI before reporting complete.

## Definition of done

- Same user-visible behavior on web and mobile for the changed scope.
- No silent no-ops on web (e.g. send button that never fires, search that logs the user out).
- Document platform-specific files only when the shared API differs; otherwise one code path.
